<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rodrigo of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 873</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Rodrigo, Count of Castile</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rodrigo was the first known count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, governing from around 850 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-i-of-asturias">Ordoño I of Asturias</a>. He resettled the abandoned town of Amaya in 860 and defended the eastern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-rodriguez-porcelos">Diego Rodríguez Porcelos</a> succeeded him and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rodrigo's parentage is not documented. Some Iberian Muslim writers mention a brother or brother-in-law of Ordoño I active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and his missions suggest he was close to the royal house. Fernández de Béthencourt made him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-asturias">Ramiro I of Asturias</a> by his second wife, Paterna, but since they did not marry until around 842 he would then have become count as a child, and many historians think this unlikely; Barrau-Dihago rejected it for lack of support in the primary sources. Another reconstruction makes him the brother of Ordoño's wife Munia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rodrigo governed the eastern march of the realm, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> called al-Qila and which later became the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, named for the castles built in a land with few natural defences. His territory, Bardulia, was then small, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, Brañosera, the Cantabrian Mountains and a line of fortifications. Castilian soldiers took Haro in 854, and in 859 Rodrigo fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-albelda">Battle of Monte Laturce</a>, where the armies of Ordoño and García Íñiguez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-musa">Musa ibn Musa</a>. Many of his settlers at Amaya were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a> fleeing persecution in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a>, and in 863 he raided as far as Talamanca de Jarama.</p><p>Emir Muhammad I struck back. In 863 his son Abd ar-Rahman and the commander Abd al-Malik ibn Abbas broke Rodrigo's defence of the gorge near Pancorbo, and in 865 an army of 20,000 took the border fortresses and defeated him at the Battle of the Morcuera near Añana. When Alfonso III was overthrown by Count Fruela Bermudez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a> in 866, Rodrigo raised an army that restored him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, and he became one of the king's closest advisers. After ending the rebellion of the Basque magnate Eglyón without a fight, he was made count of Álava, holding it until 870, the year he retook Pancorbo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rodrigo last appears in a document of 18 April 873 and died on 4 October or 5 November that year. His son Diego Rodríguez succeeded him with the king's approval, the first count in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> to inherit his possessions. His standing as the probable founding count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> led later writers to enlarge his deeds: forged charters push his rule a decade earlier than can be traced, and earlier counts named Rodrigo and Diego were invented.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rodrigo of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its first known count, <b>Rodrigo</b>, governed from around 850, and his son Diego Rodríguez Porcelos founded Burgos in 884.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-castile">County of Castile</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Asturias. Died in the County of Castilla. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
